# Question 1: Population is the large group of people/items that we want to study and learn about. The sample is the subset of the population (the actual data we have avaliable to us)
# Question 2: An item relating to a process that occurs by chance
# Question 3:
# a) The sample mean tells us the average and helps us make an inference about the population mean (mu)
# b) No, the sample is random so we might get different values
# Question 1: A Population is the big group of items/people/values that we want to study and learn about. A Sample is the subset of our population that we get data on and can actually analyze (only have 1 sample typically)
# Question 2: some placeholder for some quantity whose exact value is dependent on the outcome of a random event.
# Question 3:
# a) The sample mean (x_bar) helps us make an inference about the population mean (mu). It tells us about the average of the sample.
# b) No. Each sample will be slightly different, so the sample mean might change for each sample that we get.
# Question 4: The sample is the data that we have avliable to us (only have 1 of these). The sampling distribution is the mean of a whole bunch of different samples.
population <- rexp(10000, rate=1)
hist(population, main="Population Distribution", xlab="Value")
mean(population)
sd(population)
population <- rexp(10000, rate=1)
mean(population)
sd(population)
samples_5 <- matrix(rexp(5*1000, rate=1), nrow=5, ncol=1000)
means_5 <- apply(samples_5, MARGIN=2, FUN=mean)
hist(means_5, main="Sampling Distribution: n = 5", xlab="Sample Means")
mean(means_5)
sd(means_5)
samples_5[,1]
mean(samples_5[,1])
means_5[1]
1/sqrt(5)
